A||||9||JohnF||Pulling the pan?||||5||
Z||000000||JohnF||10-11-2001||10:23 AM||||Can the pan be pulled from an '85 V6 without pulling the engine.  My mechanic says no, this board has indicated maybe.  Anyone know?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Sage||10-11-2001||11:05 AM||||I've done it, and I'm sure there are others here that have also.  You just have to unbolt and move the starter out of the way,(don't forget to unhook the battery!), and I think take the front motor mount loose,(it's been a while since I did this), then hoist the engine up enough for the pan to clear.  I replaced rod bearings in an 86GT this way.  Have also done it with an 84 2.5.  Couple hours work.<P>Hope this helps.||||reg||1
Z||000002||PBJ||10-11-2001||12:06 PM||||if you cut away a small section of the mtr mount that is hanging up the removal of the pan.  will slide past.||||reg||1
Z||000003||kyote||10-11-2001||12:33 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JohnF:<BR><B>Can the pan be pulled from an '85 V6 without pulling the engine.  My mechanic says no, this board has indicated maybe.  Anyone know?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I just did it..on my 86 SE...<P>You need to take the motor mount out and then swing the motor mount bracket out of the way..after putting a couple of small pieces of 2x4 under the pulley/balancer.. <P>(only need to loosen the top bolt on the bracket.. remove the bottom one..)<P>also need to remove the flywheel cover in addition to the starter.. otherwise it kinda hangs up....<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroRacer||10-11-2001||04:51 PM||||Oh joy, I just finished doing this very fun, challenging, and frustrating task.  Here's how I did it.<BR>Car on Jack Stands<BR>Floor Jack and block of wood under Transmission pan.<BR>Removed passenger side mount<BR>Jiggled exhaust pipe to get pan to clear.<BR>Success.  Next time I may just cut a notch in the mount though.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Pheagey||10-11-2001||05:08 PM||||what about pulling the pan from an 88 coupe<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||JohnF||10-11-2001||05:29 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroRacer:<BR><B><BR>Floor Jack and block of wood under Transmission pan.<BR>Removed passenger side mount<BR>Jiggled exhaust pipe to get pan to clear.<BR>Success.  Next time I may just cut a notch in the mount though.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Why floor jack and wood under pan?  If so, how did you remove pan?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||FieroRacer||10-11-2001||10:52 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JohnF:<BR><B> Why floor jack and wood under pan?  If so, how did you remove pan?<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The floor jack and wood went under the Tranmission oil pan, not the engine oil pan.  Worked nicely.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Pike||10-12-2001||01:59 AM||||I just had to do this you maniacs! I rebuilt my 2.8 on the workbench and installed it on the trans without the pan or oil pump. (too much trouble to roll the engine over on the bench...I thought! stupid!, stupid!) After I put the engine back in the car and I tried to put the pan on I said to myself...stupid! stupid! Yep...they are pretty much right. I pulled the engine mount off the front and raised the engine/trans with a floor jack and wood block under the TRANS PAN. Then you have LOTs of room to work. Oh ya...starter needs to come off to do this and remove the springs from the exhaust so you can move it out of the way. It was soooo much fun! I'm so glad you get to have some too!<BR>Pike||||reg||1
Z||000009||Linx||10-14-2001||01:58 AM||||Yah- I did it the same way!<P>BUT...I notched the motor mount, because I knew I'd be back again...soon. lol.||||reg||1
